Through slices 1–32 of the build, the AI kept a log in plan.md of every decision and addition that my spec, architecture and contracts files didn’t cover. When I reviewed the summary, I saw that the contracts file was missing several pieces the code needed. Six public functions had to be added. Among them were ways to reserve a sequence number before signing an update, store encrypted private keys, record the last contact with Earth, and store manifests on the station. I also chose to add the cryptography package for Ed25519 signatures. A few existing functions changed behavior, such as only accepting sequence numbers that were reserved first. All of these will need to be folded back into contracts.md.

Building the system also exposed many smaller gaps in the spec that had to be filled. These included how IDs are numbered, how totals are rounded, how ties are broken when sorting alerts and missions, and around a dozen validation rules, like refusing duplicate barcodes or edits that change nothing. I made the bigger calls myself, such as the permission rules on Earth and how private keys are handled at sign-in. The AI made the rest when I had no preference, including most of the screen wording and the clock drift assumptions.

There are also places where the code doesn’t yet match my documents. The biggest is that the architecture’s K5 requirement isn’t met. The Earth Store runs on SQLite with no replica or failover, so NFR-008 isn’t satisfied either. Read-only screens don’t check whether the user is signed in, a couple of components use the wall clock instead of the injected one, and there are no navigation links yet. That is something I want to improve before the project is finished.
